    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I implemented for the spec:\n\n## Change summary\n\n**The cap (core of the spec)** \u2014 in `OwnerRestControllerV1.assignMembership`, the derived level is now passed through a new `capMembershipLevelByHousehold` helper:\n\n```java\nowner.setMembershipLevel(capMembershipLevelByHousehold(owner, membershipLevelForPoints(points)));\n```\n\n`capMembershipLevelByHousehold` finds the maximum `membershipLevel` among the new owner's **existing** household members (owners already carrying the same `householdId`) and caps the derived level at `maxHouseholdLevel + 1`. When the owner belongs to no household, or has no existing household member, `maxHouseholdLevel` stays `null` and the derived level is returned unchanged \u2014 i.e. no cap applies.\n\n**Enabling multiple household members** \u2014 the new test (`coreCapsLevelByHousehold`) creates two owners with the same last name + postcode (same household) but distinct telephone/email, and requires both creates to succeed. The existing `rejectDuplicate` treated *any* same-household owner as a 409 duplicate, which made the feature's premise (\"household members\" as separate owner records) impossible. Since the `identityKey` already encodes `telephone + email + householdId`, I dropped the household-only rejection so genuine re-registrations (same identity key) are still rejected, while distinct people in one household are allowed through. Updated the method's Javadoc accordingly.\n\nAll tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
